# evidence-cmd: sh -c 'cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"; d=$(mktemp -d); printf %s "" | base64 -d > "$d/e.py"; python3 "$d/e.py"; s=$?; rm -rf "$d"; exit $s'
# evidence-for: FR-030, AC-040, FR-039, US-010
# evidence-timeout: 300
#
# GRIND cycle 16, lead item L-16-1 (1). Two docstrings -- conftest.build_fixture_repo's and
# test_harness's test_fixture_head_survives_a_change_of_host_timezone -- said git dated a
# bare-date commit "at the host's ``-0600``". The measurement behind that sentence was real
# and the sentence was not: -0600 is what this host returns, not what a host returns. The
# neighbouring interpreter claims in the same two files are hedged with "here"; these two were
# written as though the offset were a property of git or of the fixture.
#
# Block [1] names the two zones before any commit is made: the host's, read from
# /etc/localtime, and the Pacific/Kiritimati the parent process exports -- 20 hours apart, so
# "the child took the host's offset" and "the child took the parent's" cannot be mistaken for
# one another. Block [2] is the case the docstring is about: the real conftest builder, every
# commit date overridden to a string carrying no offset, and env= carrying no TZ. Block [3]
# runs the same builder with TZ forwarded, which is what makes the claim a property rather
# than a reading: the recorded offset follows whatever zone the git child is in, and the
# unforwarded row is simply the host's. Block [4] prints both corrected sentences from the
# committed files, so the prose and the measurement are in one artifact.
#
# conftest.py is executed here with a stubbed pytest rather than imported through the suite,
# so the log runs under a plain python3. GI-004 bars a test module from importing a webster
# script; it says nothing about this log executing the harness whose docstring it measures.


[1] the two zones in play before a single commit is made
    host /etc/localtime resolves to:      America/Denver
    parent process TZ:                    Pacific/Kiritimati  (its own strftime %z: +1400)
    date handed to git, every commit:     '2026-08-27T16:49:56'  (no offset in the string)
    git:                                  git version 2.50.1 (Apple Git-155)

[2] FR-030 / AC-040: TZ exported in the parent, dropped by build_fixture_repo's env=
    author date git recorded:             2026-08-27T16:49:56-06:00
    committer date git recorded:          2026-08-27T16:49:56-06:00
    HEAD:                                 1c0be05e402f19d6d848a16312f9b4c22d671c28
    -> the offset git wrote is            -06:00
       the parent was holding             +1400
       so the child read neither the parent's TZ nor a constant: it read
       America/Denver, the host's own zone. On a host in another zone the same
       bare string records that host's offset instead.

[3] the same builder with TZ forwarded through env=, to show whose zone decides
    TZ reaching the git child  author date recorded        HEAD
    (none forwarded)           2026-08-27T16:49:56-06:00   1c0be05e402f19d6d848a16312f9b4c22d671c28
    Pacific/Kiritimati         2026-08-27T16:49:56+14:00   7718e1755c2484b77a0470f62452d5c401aaf44e
    America/Los_Angeles        2026-08-27T16:49:56-07:00   411d2bb1b53d3274312df1ce15d648918f549363
    UTC                        2026-08-27T16:49:56Z        98fc1e61f90f0baa101f9c8bfcf3c17d77772af8
    distinct offsets: 4   distinct HEADs: 4
    -> the offset is a property of the zone the git process is in, not of
       this machine. Unforwarded it is the host's, and the host's here is
       America/Denver at -06:00.

[4] the two sentences this measurement backs, as committed
    plugins/webster/tests/conftest.py:
      Measured: with ``TZ=Pacific/Kiritimati`` exported in the parent and dropped
      by that ``env=``, git resolved a bare date in whatever zone the child was
      in — with nothing forwarding ``TZ``, the host's own, read from
      ``/etc/localtime``. That offset is ``-0600`` on the machine this was
      measured on, against the ``+1400`` the parent was holding; on a
      differently-zoned host it is that host's offset instead, which is the whole
      of the hazard. A test written that way would compare two builds in one zone
      and report a pass.
    plugins/webster/tests/test_harness.py:
      Measured: exactly that, the commit taking the host's own offset rather than
      the ``+1400`` the parent was holding — ``-0600`` on the machine this was
      measured on, and that host's own offset on any other.

    Neither sentence now states -0600 as a property of the fixture or of
    git. Both state the property -- the bare date takes the zone the git
    child is in -- and give -0600 as the value block [2] measured here.
